'''''Ruppia cirrhosa''''' (syn. ''Ruppia spiralis''), '''spiral tasselweed''', is a species of [[aquatic plant]] in the [[Potamogetonaceae|pondweed family]], with a [[cosmopolitan distribution]], that grows in [[Brackish water|brackish]] to [[Saline water|saline]] waters. It has long, thin leaves that often form a dense layer under the surfaces of coastal ditches, lakes and lagoons. The flowers are tiny and petal-less, and produce pollen that is dispersed by the wind or floating on the water. The name refers to its ability to produce a long, coiled stalk to the fruiting inflorescence, which is not often seen.

==Description==
[[File:Spiral Tasselweed Ruppia cirrhosa (6171516651).jpg|thumb|left|Fruiting spiral tasselweed plant, showing the characteristic long peduncle and the inflated leaf sheath.]]
Spiral tasselweed is a perennial [[aquatic plant]] with a [[monopodial]] rhizome that each year produces slender, delicate stems up to about 0.6 m long, with many long branches. The leaves are all the same and typically grow entirely submerged, with blades up to about 120&nbsp;mm long by about 1&nbsp;mm wide. The tips of the leaves are minutely serrated and quite blunt. The leaf sheaths are up to 25&nbsp;mm long, open along one side and papery (hyaline) along the other. There is no ligule.<ref name=pondweeds>{{cite book |last=Preston |first=C.D. |title=Pondweeds of Great Britain and Ireland |date=1995 |publisher=Botanical Society of the British Isles |location=London |isbn=0-901158-24-0}}</ref>

The inflorescences arise singly at the leaf axils, within the distinctively inflated sheaths of the [[Bract#Involucral_bracts|involucral]] leaves. Each inflorescence contains two flowers close together at the tip of a stalk, or peduncle, which can be up to 300&nbsp;mm long. Each flower has two stamens, each with two lobes, thus appearing to have four yellow, round anthers. In the middle of this group of four anthers there is cluster of 2-8 [[Gynoecium#Carpels|carpels]], which occur on very short stalks that lengthen as the fruit matures until they get to about 32&nbsp;mm in length. In spiral tasselweed the peduncle often becomes coiled at this time but, even if not coiled, it is usually at least twice as long as the individual fruit stalks, and sometimes as much as ten times as long. The flowers typically occur on the surface, in which case the pollen is dispersed by water currents,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Verhoeven |first=J. T. A. |date=1979-01-01 |title=The ecology of Ruppia-dominated communities in western Europe. I. Distribution of Ruppia representatives in relation to their autecology |journal=Aquatic Botany |volume=6 |pages=197–267 |doi=10.1016/0304-3770(79)90064-0 |bibcode=1979AqBot...6..197V |issn=0304-3770}}</ref> or raised into the air, when they can be wind-pollinated.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kitchener |first=G.D. |title=Kent Rare Plant Register |year=2025 |url=https://archive.org/details/kent-rpr-2025}}</ref>
[[File:Ruppia spiralis pollen.jpg|thumb|Spiral tasselweed flowering on the surface of a ditch.]]

The fruits are flask-shaped, up to about 3.5&nbsp;mm long, and brown or grey when mature, with tiny reddish warts on the surface. They are almost symmetrical in outline, unlike the irregularly shaped fruits of [[Ruppia maritima|beaked tasselweed]].<ref name=Sell1>{{cite book |last1=Sell |first1=Peter |last2=Murrell |first2=Gina |title=Flora of Great Britain and Ireland, vol. 1 |date=2018 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |location=Cambridge|isbn=978-0-521-55335-3}}</ref><ref name=Schou>{{Cite book |last1=Schou |first1=J.C. |last2=Moeslund |first2=B. |last3=van de Weyer |first3=K. |last4=Lansdown |first4=R.V. |last5=Wiegleb |first5=G. |last6=Holm |first6=P. |last7=Baastrup-Spohr |first7=L. |last8=Sand-Jensen |first8=K. |display-authors=1 |title=Aquatic Plants of Northern and Central Europe including Britain and Ireland |publisher=Princeton University Press |year=2023 |isbn=978-0-691-25101-1 |location=Princeton, N.J.}}</ref>
[[File:Ruppia spiralis fruit.jpg|thumb|The fruit are slightly asymmetrical, but less so than in ''R. maritima''.]]

==Identification==
[[File:Ruppia spiralis leaf tip.jpg|thumb|The leaf tip is rounded and minutely serrated.]]
Great care is needed in identifying the various species of tasselweed. In north-west Europe (including Ireland and Britain) there are only two wild species: spiral tasselweed and [[Ruppia maritima|beaked tasselweed]]. Only spiral tasselweed produces a coiled peduncle, but this is rarely seen. The relative lengths of the peduncle and the fruit stalks are more reliable: spiral tasselweed has peduncles up to 4&nbsp;cm - longer than the fruit stalks, which are up to 3.2&nbsp;cm, whereas beaked tasselweed has peduncles up to 2.6&nbsp;cm - shorter than the stalks, which are up to 3.5&nbsp;cm.<ref name=Stace>{{Cite book |last=Stace |first=C.A. |title=New Flora of the British Isles |publisher=C & M Floristics |year=2019 |isbn=978-1-5272-2630-2 |edition=4th |location=Middlewood Green}}</ref>

==Taxonomy and nomenclature==
In 1753 Linnaeus coined the name ''Ruppia'' for the genus, but the only species he recognised was ''Ruppia maritima''.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Linné |first1=Carl von |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/14554 |title=Caroli a Linné Species plantarum exhibentes plantas rite cognitas, ad genera relatas, cum differentiis specificis, nominibus trivialibus, synonymis selectis, locis natalibus, secundum systema sexuale digestas |last2=Linné |first2=Carl von |last3=Willdenow |first3=Karl Ludwig |last4=Link |first4=Heinrich Friedrich |last5=Nauck |first5=Gottfried Carl |last6=Schwaegrichen |first6=F. |date=1797 |publisher=Impensis G.C. Nauk |edition=Editio quarta, post Reichardianam quinta adjectis vegetabilibus hucusque cognitis, curante Carolo Ludovico Willdenow |volume=t.1:pt.2 (1797) |location=Berolini}}</ref> It was not until 1827 that [[Barthélemy Charles Joseph Dumortier|Dumortier]] realised that there were two species, and he named spiral tasselweed ''Ruppia spiralis.'' However, [[Vincenzo Petagna|Petagna]] had also named the same plant ''Buccaferrea cirrhosa'' in 1787 (although it was not published until 1826). Either way, that pre-dates Dumortier's discovery. The genus ''Buccaferrea'' is clearly not needed, so the combination ''Ruppia cirrhosa'' was finally published by Loreto Grande in 1918.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Grande, Loreto {{!}} International Plant Names Index |url=https://www.ipni.org/a/3324-1 |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=www.ipni.org}}</ref> These overlapping dates leave some uncertainty about which is the correct name to apply, but the general consensus at present is that ''Ruppia cirrhosa'' is best.<ref name=gbif>{{Cite web |title=Ruppia cirrhosa (Petagna) Grande |url=https://www.gbif.org/species/2863987 |access-date=24 September 2025 |website=Global Biodiversity Information Forum}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Ruppia cirrhosa (Petagna) Grande {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:699838-1 |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=Plants of the World Online |language=en}}</ref> However, a recent (2017) study of herbarium specimens suggests that Petagna's tasselweed might actually have been ''Ruppia maritima'', in which case Dumortier's ''R. spiralis'' would be the correct name for the new species.<ref name="Ito et al. 2017">{{cite journal|author1=Ito, Y.|author2=Ohi-Toma, T.|author3=Nepi, C.|author4=Santangelo, A.|author5=Stinca, A.|author6=Tanaka, N.|author7=Murata, J.|year=2017|doi=10.12705/661.11|title=Towards a better understanding of the ''Ruppia maritima'' complex (Ruppiaceae): Notes on the correct application and typification of the names ''R.&nbsp;cirrhosa'' and ''R. spiralis''|journal=[[Taxon (journal)|Taxon]]|volume=66|issue=1|pages=167–171 |bibcode=2017Taxon..66..167I }}</ref>

''Ruppia cirrhosa'' has a complex taxonomy and is sometimes considered an aggregate of numerous taxa. For example, the western Mediterranean plant ''Ruppia drepanensis'' <small>Tineo</small> could be a subspecies. Some authors have suggested that ''R.&nbsp;cirrhosa'' and ''R.&nbsp;spiralis'' are actually different species rather than synonyms.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=den Hartog |first1=C. |last2=Triest |first2=L. |title=A profound view and discourse on the typification and status of three confused taxa: Ruppia maritima, R. spiralis and R. cirrhosa |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338610328 |journal=Botanica Marina |year=2020 |volume=63 |issue=3 |pages=229–239 |doi=10.1515/bot-2019-0045 |bibcode=2020BoMar..63..229D }}</ref> The complexity is caused in part because ''R. cirrhosa'' occurs as diploid (2''n''&nbsp;=&nbsp;20), triploid (2''n''&nbsp;=&nbsp;30) or tetraploid (2''n''&nbsp;=&nbsp;40) populations. It also hybridises with ''R. maritima.''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mannino |first1=Anna M. |last2=Menéndez |first2=M. |last3=Obrador |first3=B. |last4=Sfriso |first4=A. |last5=Triest |first5=L. |date=2015-07-01 |title=The genus Ruppia L. (Ruppiaceae) in the Mediterranean region: An overview |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304377015000182 |journal=Aquatic Botany |volume=124 |pages=1–9 |doi=10.1016/j.aquabot.2015.02.005 |bibcode=2015AqBot.124....1M |hdl=10278/3661481 |issn=0304-3770|hdl-access=free }}</ref>

The generic name ''Ruppia'' was given in honour of the German botanist [[Heinrich Bernhard Ruppius|Heinrich Bernhard Rupp]], while the specific name ''cirrhosa'' is from the Ancient Greek word [https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cirrhosis κιρρός] (kirros), which means tawny.

==Distribution and status==
Most sources suggest that spiral tasselweed has a cosmopolitan, circumpolar distribution,<ref name="gbif"/><ref name="powo"/> but there is some debate about this. Generally, it is found only in brackish to saline coastal locations, but North American plants (sometimes referred to as ''R.&nbsp;occidentalis'') grow in freshwater inland lakes.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ruppia cirrhosa - FNA |url=https://floranorthamerica.org/Ruppia_cirrhosa |access-date=2025-09-25 |website=floranorthamerica.org}}</ref><ref name="Ito et al. 2017"/>

Its global status has been assessed by the IUCN as LC, or "[[Least-concern species|least concern]]", which means there is no evidence for a serious decline.<ref>{{Cite web |last7=Callabine |first7=A. |title=Spiral Ditchgrass |url=https://www.iucnredlist.org/species/164280/5808308 |access-date=25 September 2025 |website=The IUCN Red List of threatened species |date=23 October 2007 }}</ref> However, it is rare in some countries and regions and can have a local conservation status such as VU ([[Vulnerable species|vulnerable]]) in Brittany, extinct in lower Normandy, and NT ([[Near-threatened species|near threatened]] in Britain.<ref name=Guillemot>{{Cite book |last=Guillemot |first=Vincent |title=Flore du Massif armoricain et ses marges |publisher=Editions Biotope |year=2023 |isbn=978-2-36662-301-7 |location=Mèze |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=PlantAtlas |url=https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.7mm53b |access-date=2025-09-25 |website=plantatlas2020.org}}</ref>

For conservation purposes, spiral tasselweed is generally considered an [[axiophyte]], or indicator of good environmental conditions.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Axiophytes – Botanical Society of Britain & Ireland |url=https://bsbi.org/axiophytes |access-date=2025-09-28 |language=en-GB}}</ref> There is evidence to suggest that its presence improved the diversity of invertebrates and the nutritional quality of the substrate in coastal lagoons.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Munari |first1=Cristina |last2=Casoni |first2=Elia |last3=Cozzula |first3=Cinzia |last4=Pasculli |first4=Alessandra |last5=Pezzi |first5=Marco |last6=Sciuto |first6=Katia |last7=Sfriso |first7=Andrea Augusto |last8=Sfriso |first8=Adriano |last9=Mistri |first9=Michele |date=2023-06-07 |title=The Ecological Role of Ruppia cirrhosa (Petagna) Grande in a Choked Lagoon |journal=Water |language=en |volume=15 |issue=12 |page=2162 |doi=10.3390/w15122162 |bibcode=2023Water..15.2162M |doi-access=free |issn=2073-4441 |hdl=10278/5032640 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

==Habitat and ecology==
[[File:Ruppia spiralis lagoon.jpg|thumb|A meadow of spiral tasselweed in a lagoon at Minnis Bay, Kent.]]
Although spiral tasselweed often grows on its own, it is occasionally found with other plants such as the eelgrasses ''[[Zostera marina]]'' and ''[[Zostera noltii|Z.&nbsp;noltii]]'' - as at The Fleet, Dorset.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Stewart |first1=A. |last2=Pearman |first2=D.A. |last3=Preston |first3=C.D. |title=Scarce Plants in Britain |date=1994 |publisher=Joint Nature Conservation Committee |location=Peterborough}}</ref> Within the [[British National Vegetation Classification]] it is probably restricted to the SM2 ''Ruppia maritima'' community, although there was much confusion between the two species of tasselweed in the 1980s, and the description  of SM2 could usefully be reworked.<ref name=Rodwell5>{{cite book |last1=Rodwell |first1=J.S. |title=British Plant Communities, vol. 5: Maritime communities and vegetation of open habitats|year=2000 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |location=Cambridge |isbn=0-521-39167-9}}</ref> Most sources seem to suggest that spiral tasselweed is strictly coastal, but not a marine plant.<ref name=Atlas>{{cite book |last1=Preston |first1=C.D. |last2=Pearman |first2=D.A. |last3=Dines |first3=T.D. |title=New Atlas of the British and Irish Flora |date=2002 |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=Oxford |isbn=0-19-851067-5}}</ref><ref name="Guillemot" />
[[File:Ruppia cirrhosa underwater.jpg|thumb|Individual plants arise from rhizomes buried in the substrate.]]

Its [[Indicator value|Ellenberg values]] in Britain are L = 7, F = 12, R = 7, N = 4, and S = 4, which reflect its habitat requirements for waterbodies in reasonably sunny places with neutral soils and low fertility, and brackish conditions.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Hill|first1=M.O.|last2=Mountford|first2=J.O.|last3=Roy|first3=D.B.|last4=Bunce|first4=R.G.H.|title=Ellenberg's indicator values for British plants. ECOFACT Volume 2. Technical Annex|date=1999|publisher=Institute of Terrestrial Ecology|isbn=1-870393-48-1|url=http://nora.nerc.ac.uk/6411/1/ECOFACT2a.pdf|access-date=27 September 2025}}</ref>

The only insect association recorded in Europe (and probably in Britain, where it has been found on ''R.&nbsp;maritima'', possibly including ''R.&nbsp;cirrhosa'') is with the [[cercozoan]] ''[[Tetramyxa parasitica]]'', which creates spherical galls in the stems of infested plants.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ruppia cirrhosa – Plant Parasites of Europe |url=https://bladmineerders.nl/host-plants/plantae/spermatopsida/angiosperma/monocots/alismatales/ruppiaceae/ruppia/ruppia-cirrhosa/ |access-date=2025-09-27 |website=bladmineerders.nl}}</ref><ref name=redfern>{{cite journal |last1=Redfern |first1=Margaret |last2=Shirley |first2=Peter |title=British Plant Galls |journal=Field Studies |date=2002 |volume=10 |pages=207–531 |isbn=1-85153-214-5}}</ref>

